        "t": "chr",
        "v": [
          "This module classifies repository files and, for tabular data files available locally, extracts each column's type and summary statistics.",
          "#### Data Tree",
          "Where the files sit within each repository. Each file is followed by how it was classified, which study it belongs to.",
          "**Repository: https://osf.io/dupcl**",
          "```\n└── data/\n    └── dupclass.csv  📊 data ex1\n```",
          "#### Data Files",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## dupclass.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(d = structure(18262:18273, class = c(\"IDate\", \n\"Date\")), d = 1:12, n = c(3L, 6L, 9L, 12L, 15L, 18L, 21L, 24L, \n27L, 30L, 33L, 36L)), row.names = c(NA, 12L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 10, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "#### Descriptives Overview\n\nWe found 0 columns with at least one missing value.",
          "::: {.panel-tabset}\n\n## dupclass.csv\n\n\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(Column = c(\"d\", \"d\", \"n\"), Representation = c(\"datetime\", \n\"numeric\", \"numeric\"), Level = c(\"nominal\", \"ratio\", \"ratio\"), \n    Concept = c(\"date\", \"\", \"\"), Role = c(\"timestamp\", \"measure\", \n    \"measure\"), Unit = c(\"\", \"\", \"\"), Rows = c(12L, 12L, 12L), \n    \"% Missing\" = c(0, 0, 0), \"N Unique\" = c(12L, 12L, 12L), \n    Mean = c(NA, 6.5, 19.5), SD = c(NA, 3.606, 10.817), Min = c(NA, \n    1, 3), Max = c(NA, 12, 36)), row.names = c(NA, 3L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 25, FALSE)\n```\n\n\n:::",
          "This module runs automated data-quality checks (outliers, miscoded missing values, empty and constant columns, SPSS filter variables, category casing, problematic column names, mixed text encodings, spreadsheet formatting) on the extracted data files.",
          "We examined 3 columns across 1 data file.",
          "#### Issues Identified",
          "1 file/column combination has at least one issue.",
          "\n```{r}\n#| echo: false\n\n\n# table data --------------------------------------\ntable <- structure(list(File = \"dupclass.csv\", Column = \"d\", Issues = \"<span title='Column name becomes \\\"d\\\" when special characters are replaced, the same as 1 other identically named column: tools that sanitize names (R&#39;s make.names(), SPSS/SAS/Stata import, codebook section links) cannot tell these columns apart.'>👥 Colliding column names — Column name becomes \\\"d\\\" when special characters are replaced, the s...<\/span>\"), row.names = c(NA, \n-1L), class = \"data.frame\")\n\n# display table -----------------------------------\nmetacheck::report_table(table, \"auto\", 20, FALSE)\n```\n",
          "#### Careless Responding",
          "No file could be screened for careless responding.",
          "**What this does not cover.** Only 0 of 1 data file could be screened: the rest had no detectable multi-item scale block, or too few respondents. Even in a screened file, these checks only look at multi-item rating scales — the only place where a repeated or flat answer pattern is interpretable. Single questions, open text, demographics and any item outside a detected scale block are never examined. A respondent can therefore answer most of a survey carelessly and still not be flagged here, and finding nothing is not evidence that a dataset is free of careless responding."
        ]
